.TH ED 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
ed \- line-oriented text editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ed
.RB [ \-s ]
.RI [ \-p " string" ]
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ed
is a line-oriented text editor. It keeps an in-memory buffer of lines,
loaded at startup from
.I file
if given (an empty buffer otherwise), and then repeatedly reads one
line from standard input as a command, executes it, and (unless
.B \-s
was given) writes a prompt string first if prompting is enabled.
.PP
Almost every command may be preceded by an address, or a pair of
addresses separated by
.B ,
or
.BR \; ,
selecting the line or range of lines the command acts on; most default
to the current line when no address is given. A command that does not
accept an address at all
.RB ( e ,
.BR E ,
.BR f ,
.BR q ,
.BR Q ,
.BR u ,
.BR H ,
.BR h ,
.BR P ,
and
.BR ! )
reports an error if given one anyway. The one exception is
.BR = ,
which is allowed to resolve to address 0 on an empty buffer, since it
only ever reports a number and never touches buffer content.
.PP
A command line consisting of just an address (no command letter at
all) prints the addressed line(s), setting the current line to the
last one printed; with no address either, it prints the line after the
current one.
.PP
Whenever a command cannot be carried out as written, a single
.B ?
is written to standard output; if help mode
(see the
.B H
command) is on, an explanation follows on the next line.
.SH ADDRESSES
.TP
.B .
The current line.
.TP
.B $
The last line of the buffer.
.TP
.I number
An absolute line number.
0 is a valid address only where a specific command's own description
below allows it.
.TP
.BI ' x
The line marked with the lowercase letter
.I x
(see the
.B k
command).
.TP
.IB / bre /
Search forward, starting after the current line and wrapping around to
the beginning of the buffer if necessary, for the next line whose
contents the basic regular expression
.I bre
matches. An empty
.B //
reuses whichever regular expression was most recently used by any
search or
.BR s /// .
.TP
.IB ? bre ?
The same, but searching backward and wrapping around to the end of the
buffer.
.TP
.RI [ addr ]+ n ", " [ addr ]- n
Relative to the address component before it (or to the current line,
if there is none):
.I n
lines later or earlier. A bare
.B +
or
.B \-
means 1. A bare
.I n
with no sign, following another address component, also means
.BR + n .
Any number of these may be chained.
.PP
Two addresses joined with
.B ,
form a range; a bare
.B ,
means
.BR 1,$ .
Two addresses joined with
.B \;
form the same kind of range, except that the current line is set to
the first address before the second one is evaluated, so a relative
second address is relative to the first, not to whatever the current
line was beforehand; a bare
.B \;
means
.BR .,$ .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.RI [ addr ] a
Append text (see TEXT INPUT below) after the addressed line (default
the current line; 0 is valid, meaning before the first line). The
current line becomes the last line entered.
.TP
.RI [ addr ] i
Insert text before the addressed line (default the current line; 0 is
treated as 1). The current line becomes the last line entered.
.TP
.RI [ addr1 , addr2 ] c
Change: delete the addressed lines (default the current line) and
insert text (see TEXT INPUT below) in their place.
.TP
.RI [ addr1 , addr2 ] d [ p | l | n ]
Delete the addressed lines (default the current line). An optional
trailing
.BR p ", " l ", or " n
prints the new current line, in the same format those letters give the
.B p
command below.
.TP
.RI [ addr1 , addr2 ] j [ p | l | n ]
Join the addressed lines (default the current line and the line after
it) into a single line, replacing them. Joining a range of only one
line leaves it unchanged. The trailing letter behaves as for
.BR d .
.TP
.RI [ addr1 , addr2 ] m " addr3" [ p | l | n ]
Move the addressed lines (default the current line) to just after
.I addr3
.RI ( addr3
may be 0, meaning before the first line, but may not fall within the
range being moved). The trailing letter behaves as for
.BR d .
.TP
.RI [ addr1 , addr2 ] t " addr3" [ p | l | n ]
Like
.BR m ,
but copies the addressed lines instead of moving them; the source
lines are left in place.
.TP
.RI [ addr1 , addr2 ] p
Print the addressed lines (default the current line) plainly, setting
the current line to the last one printed.
.TP
.RI [ addr1 , addr2 ] n
Like
.BR p ,
but precedes each line with its line number and a tab.
.TP
.RI [ addr1 , addr2 ] l
Like
.BR p ,
but in a visually unambiguous form: non-printable bytes are backslash
escaped, and each line ends with an unescaped
.BR $ .
.TP
.RI [ addr ] =
Print the line number of the addressed line (default the last line of
the buffer; 0 for an empty buffer).
.TP
.RI [ addr1 , addr2 ] s / bre / replacement / flags
Substitute
.I replacement
for text matching the basic regular expression
.I bre
on each addressed line (default the current line). Any byte may be
used as the delimiter in place of
.BR / .
.I flags
is zero or more of: a decimal occurrence number (default 1); a
trailing
.B g
(replace every match from that occurrence onward, instead of only
that one); and a trailing
.BR p ", " l ", or " n
(print the last line actually changed, in the format those letters
give
.B p
above). An empty
.B //
reuses the last regular expression used by any search or
.BR s /// ;
a replacement text of exactly
.B %
reuses the previous replacement text. The replacement may contain
.B &
(the whole matched text) and
.BI \e n
for
.I n
1\(en9 (text matched by the
.IR n th
parenthesized subexpression of
.IR bre ).
.TP
.RI [ addr ] k x
Mark the addressed line (default the current line) with the lowercase
letter
.IR x ,
so it can later be addressed as
.BI ' x .
.TP
.B u
Undo the effect of the most recently executed buffer-modifying command
(one of
.BR a ", " c ", " d ", " g ", " i ", " j ", " m ", " r ", " s ", " t ", " u ", " v ", " G ", " V ;
one whole
.BR g / v / G / V
invocation counts as a single unit). Running
.B u
again undoes the undo itself; only one level of undo is kept.
.TP
.BI e\  file
Replace the buffer with the contents of
.IR file .
Refuses once, with an error, if the current buffer has unsaved
changes; running
.B e
a second time with the same argument proceeds anyway.
With no argument, reloads the remembered filename.
.I file
becomes the new remembered filename either way.
.TP
.BI E\  file
Like
.BR e ,
but never refuses over unsaved changes.
.TP
.BI e\ ! command\c
,\  \c
.BI E\ ! command
Run
.I command
through the shell and load its output into the buffer instead of a
file, with the same unsaved-changes behavior as
.B e
or
.B E
respectively. A
.B %
anywhere in
.I command
expands to the remembered filename;
.B \e%
is a literal
.BR % .
.TP
.BI f\  file
With an argument, set the remembered filename to
.I file
without loading anything. With no argument, print the remembered
filename.
.TP
.RI [ addr ] r " file"
Read
.I file
(or the remembered filename, if none is given) and insert its contents
after the addressed line (default the last line; 0 is valid, meaning
before the first line).
.TP
.RI [ addr ] r\ ! command
Run
.I command
through the shell and insert its output the same way.
.TP
.RI [ addr1 , addr2 ] w " file"
Write the addressed lines (default the whole buffer) to
.I file
(or the remembered filename, if none is given). If
.I file
is the first filename ever associated with this buffer and the whole
buffer was written, the buffer is marked as having no unsaved changes.
.TP
.RI [ addr1 , addr2 ] w\ ! command
Pipe the addressed lines to
.IR command 's
standard input instead of writing to a file.
.TP
.B !command
Run
.I command
through the shell (with
.B %
and
.B \e%
expanded as above). With no argument, repeats the last
.B !
command given.
.TP
.B H
Toggle whether an explanation follows each
.B ?
error report.
.TP
.B h
Print the explanation for the most recent error, regardless of whether
.B H
is currently on.
.TP
.B P
Toggle whether a prompt string is printed before reading each command.
.TP
.B q
Quit. Refuses once, with an error, if the buffer has unsaved changes
(except under
.B \-s ,
where there is no interactive user to give a second chance to, so
.B q
always quits immediately); running
.B q
a second time in a row proceeds anyway.
.TP
.B Q
Quit unconditionally, even with unsaved changes.
.TP
.RI [ addr1 , addr2 ] g / bre / command\-list
For every line in the addressed range (default the whole buffer) that
.I bre
matches, mark it; then execute
.I command\-list
(one or more commands, one per line, a line continued onto the next
with a trailing backslash; an empty
.I command\-list
means a bare
.BR p )
against each marked line in turn, in line order.
.BR a ", " i ", " c ,
and nested
.BR g / v / G / V
commands are not permitted inside
.IR command\-list .
.TP
.RI [ addr1 , addr2 ] v / bre / command\-list
Like
.BR g ,
but for the lines
.I bre
does
.B not
match.
.TP
.RI [ addr1 , addr2 ] G / bre /
Interactive form of
.BR g :
for each matching line in turn, print it, then read one command line
from standard input and execute it against that line; a bare
.B &
repeats the last command executed this way.
.TP
.RI [ addr1 , addr2 ] V / bre /
The interactive form of
.BR v .
.SH TEXT INPUT
.BR a ", " i ", and " c
collect the text to insert (or change to) from the following lines of
input, up to but not including a line that consists of exactly a
single
.BR . ,
which is required and is not itself part of the text.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-p " string"
Set the command prompt to
.I string
and turn prompting on, as if
.B P
had been typed once at startup. Without
.BR \-p ,
prompting starts off, and the default prompt (if it is ever turned on
with
.BR P )
is
.BR * .
.TP
.B \-s
Suppress the byte-count messages
.BR e ", " E ", " r ", and " w
would otherwise print, and make
.B q
quit immediately without the unsaved-changes confirmation described
under the
.B q
command above.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I file
A pathname to load into the buffer at startup. If it does not exist
yet, the buffer starts empty, the name is remembered, and this is
treated as a normal condition, not an error. If no
.I file
operand is given, the buffer starts empty with no remembered filename.
.SH APPLICATION USAGE
The following, present in some other
.B ed
implementations, are not implemented here:
an
.B W
(append-write) command;
a
.B #
comment command;
a
.B %
address synonym for
.B 1,$
(use
.B 1,$
or a bare
.B ,
instead).
.PP
Receiving SIGHUP between top-level commands does not necessarily
terminate the process running
.BR ed :
if the buffer has unsaved changes, it is written, on a best-effort
basis, to a file named
.B ed.hup
in the current directory, and command reading then stops, as if
.B Q
had been given. This is noticed only between top-level commands, not
while a lengthy
.BR g / v / G / V
command-list is running or while
.B ed
is waiting for text input.
.PP
Within a
.BR g / v
command-list, if a sub-command shifts or deletes other lines still
waiting to be visited in that invocation's own set of matched lines,
the line numbers of those still-pending lines are kept accurate, and
any whose line was deleted are skipped; however, a still-pending
line's own content being rewritten in place by an unrelated
substitution is not separately detected, so it is still visited even
though its content may no longer be what originally matched.
.PP
The
.B l
command does not fold long lines at any particular width; each
addressed line always produces exactly one
.B l
-formatted output line, however long.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
.B ed
ran to completion without any command ever failing.
.TP
.B 1
At least one command failed (reported with a
.BR ? )
at some point during the run, even if the run subsequently continued
and completed normally.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sed (1),
.BR grep (1)
